TU1NZ / WANT ME SEEN
S12.1 R9 ROOT-GIT WORKTREE TRANSITION DIAGNOSIS
Date: 2026-10-03 Europe/Berlin

Classification: SOURCE_CONTRACT_GAP
Mutation during diagnosis: none
R9 deployment retries: none
R9 recovery retries after diagnosis: none

Observed durable state

- The only R9 deployment attempt and the only reviewed R9 recovery both
  stopped fail-closed with S12_1_RECOVERY_WORKTREE_BARRIER_RED.
- The deployment attempt marker is present with attempt=1 and a bound release
  repository state. The pre-deployment backup is present.
- Both canonical repositories retain the root-owned .git guard and the
  root-only .git.s12-1-recovery directory.
- Application journal: 669 records; 393 remained sealed, 238 journaled inodes
  were replaced by root Git, and 38 same journaled inodes retained their exact
  root-Git source write mode instead of the sealed mode.
- Control journal: 210 records; 207 old index paths are absent after the
  checked-out transition, one inode was replaced, and two same journaled
  inodes were left in a root-Git normalized/source mode instead of the sealed
  mode.
- The current Git index enumerations were readable through the quarantined
  recovery Git directories. No repository guard was released.

Root cause

The write barrier correctly accepted root-owned replacement inodes only when
they already rejected group/other writes. It did not re-seal same journaled
inodes after a successful root Git body when Git had restored the source mode
or normalized its non-executable permission class. The context asserted the
post-body barrier before re-running the sealing transition, so the first R9
attempt stopped and the canonical recovery encountered the same resumable
state before entering its restore body.

Required correction

- Accept only the same journaled inode, root owner, recorded group, matching
  executable class and either the exact recorded mode or a mode already closed
  to group/other writes.
- Require the original bound xattr/ACL state before changing anything.
- For an owner-only path, recognize only the byte-exact POSIX ACL produced
  when Linux chmod changes the user-object, mask and other entries of the
  journal-derived generated owner ACL; then rebuild the exact generated ACL.
- Immediately restore the exact journal-derived sealed mode/ACL.
- Defer a journaled pathname whose device/inode changed to the current tracked
  path pass; accept only a root-owned, single-link file or directory already
  closed to group/other writers.
- Re-run the worktree seal after the root Git body and before post-body identity
  and write-barrier acceptance.
- Continue rejecting an unjournaled replacement that is writable by group or
  other, an executable-class change, an ownership/group mismatch, an xattr/ACL
  mismatch, a changed inode presented as journaled, or any non-regular/
  non-directory tracked object.

Safety outcome

The R9 freeze remains immutable evidence and is not retried. The server stays
guarded until a later reviewed controller performs the canonical recovery.
Adult media, real AVS/identity, payments, publishing, controlled beta and
production remain closed.
